    "evidence_text": "For our study, we used the number of retweets to single-out those that went viral within our sample.\n\nTweets within that subset (viral tweets hereafter) are varied and relate to different topics.\n\nAs indicated, our initial interest is to characterize tweets containing fake news that went viral (as they are the most harmful ones, as they reach a wider audience), and understand how it differs from other viral tweets (that do not contain fake news).\n\nFor our study, we consider that a tweet went viral if it was retweeted more than 1000 times.\n\nWithin our sample, we identified 1327 tweets that went viral (retweeted more than 1000 times by the 8th of November 2016) produced by 643 users.\n\nFinally, the number of hashtags used in viral fake news appears to be larger than those in other viral tweets.\n\nHowever, once again, we were not able to find any statistical difference between the average number of hashtags in a viral tweet and the average number of hashtags in viral fake news.\n\nOur aim is to advance the understanding of which features might be characteristic of viral tweets containing fake news in comparison with viral tweets without fake news.",
